.Dd July 30, 2026
.Dt CLOVE.CONF 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm clove.conf
.Nd configuration file for cloved
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
configures
.Xr cloved 8 .
It is read from
.Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/clove/clove.conf
.Pq or Pa ~/.config/clove/clove.conf
unless
.Fl c
names another file.
.Pp
An absent or empty file is the correct, working configuration.
Every key exists to
.Em deviate
from a sane default, never to enable basic function: a fresh install against a
default-configured local router downloads a torrent with no configuration
written at all.
.Pp
The format is one
.Ar key value
pair per line.
Keys and values are separated by whitespace.
Blank lines are ignored, and a
.Sq #
begins a comment that runs to the end of the line.
There is no nesting, no quoting, and no include mechanism.
.Pp
An unknown key is a fatal error, as is a key given twice or a key with no
value.
A typo must fail startup loudly rather than be silently ignored; use
.Ic cloved -C
to check a file before restarting a running daemon.
.Sh KEYWORDS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic sam_address Ar host:port
Address of the router's SAMv3 bridge.
Default:
.Pa 127.0.0.1:7656 .
.Pp
The address must be loopback
.Pq Pa 127.0.0.1 , Pa localhost , Pa [::1] .
.Pp
A unix-socket path is refused: clove speaks SAM over TCP only, and accepting
a path it could not dial meant
.Nm cloved Fl C
approving a configuration that then left the daemon with no router.
.Pa localhost
is the only hostname clove accepts anywhere: there is no DNS resolution.
Exotic loopback spellings such as
.Pa 127.0.0.2
are refused rather than given the benefit of the doubt.
.It Ic i_know_sam_is_remote Ar yes | Ar no
Acknowledge that a non-loopback
.Ic sam_address
is intended.
Default:
.Ar no .
.Pp
This is dangerous and deliberately ugly to type.
Traffic between clove and a remote SAM bridge is unprotected: anyone who can
see that link sees which destinations you talk to, defeating the point of
running over I2P.
.Pp
It is currently of no use: this build dials
.Pa 127.0.0.1
and nothing else, so a remote address is refused whether or not the flag is
set, rather than being accepted and quietly redirected to the local router.
The flag is kept so an existing configuration still parses, and so the
refusal can say what was meant.
.It Ic data_dir Ar path
Absolute path of the state directory: destination keys, per-torrent resume
data, the API token, and downloaded files.
Default:
.Pa $XDG_DATA_HOME/clove ,
that is
.Pa ~/.local/share/clove .
.It Ic api_socket Ar path
Absolute path of the unix socket serving the local HTTP API.
Default:
.Pa $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/clove.sock ,
falling back to
.Pa <data_dir>/clove.sock
when
.Ev XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
is unset.
.It Ic ephemeral Ar yes | Ar no
Use a transient I2P destination instead of persisting the client's keys, so
the identity differs on every start.
Default:
.Ar no .
.Pp
A transient identity is unlinkable across restarts, at the cost of losing
your standing in swarms and having to re-announce as a new peer each time.
.It Ic preallocate Ar yes | Ar no
Grow each of a torrent's files to its full length when the torrent is added,
instead of letting the file stay sparse and fill in as pieces arrive.
Default:
.Ar no .
.Pp
Preallocation trades disk up front for two things: a download that cannot
fail part-way through with
.Er ENOSPC
because the space was already claimed, and files that are laid out more
contiguously, which matters on rotational disks and for playing media while
it downloads.
Leave it off on a filesystem where sparse files are cheap and free space is
tight, and on one that does not support sparse files it changes nothing —
the space is taken either way.
.It Ic peer_limit Ar count
The greatest number of peer connections
.Xr cloved 8
will hold across
.Em every
torrent at once.
Default:
.Ar 200 .
.Pp
Every peer is an I2P stream on the one SAM session the whole client shares, so
this is a ceiling on that session as much as on peers.
The default is the concurrency that has actually been measured — 200 streams
on one i2pd session, with connect latency independent of how many were open —
rather than a round number, because above it we would be guessing with the
session every torrent depends on.
A session that wedges takes all of them down, not the torrent that overspent.
.Pp
Raise it if you have measured your router happily doing more; lower it on a
small or busy router, or when clove is sharing one with other applications.
.It Ic torrent_peer_limit Ar count
The greatest number of peer connections any single torrent will hold.
Default:
.Ar 50 .
.Pp
Applied underneath
.Ic peer_limit ,
which the sum is still bound by: this one stops a single busy torrent taking
the whole allowance, and is what an operator running many torrents at once
should lower.
Both keys take a number from 1 to 10000.
.Pp
There is deliberately no fair share between torrents.
With the defaults, four saturated torrents can hold the entire
.Ic peer_limit
and a fifth waits for peers to churn; if that is your situation, lower
.Ic torrent_peer_limit
rather than raising
.Ic peer_limit .
.It Ic max_active_downloads Ar count
How many incomplete torrents run at once.
Default:
.Ar 3 .
.Pp
The rest are
.Sy queued :
listed and persisted, but holding no peers, no tunnels and no threads.
Three because the scarce thing on I2P is tunnels rather than bandwidth — three
torrents each holding a healthy peer set finish sooner, one after another, than
twenty sharing the same ceiling and all crawling.
.It Ic max_active_seeds Ar count
How many complete torrents seed at once.
Default:
.Ar 5 .
.Pp
A separate allowance from
.Ic max_active_downloads ,
which is what stops a queue of downloads from also stopping the client seeding
what it has already finished.
When a download completes it moves from one allowance to the other, freeing its
download slot for whatever is next in the queue.
.Pp
The queue order is the order torrents were added, and it does not change:
resuming a paused torrent returns it to its own place rather than to the back,
which can displace a newer torrent that had been promoted in its absence.
That torrent keeps everything it downloaded; what it loses is its peers and an
announce.
.Ic clove start
puts one torrent at the head of the queue when that is not what you want.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
The recommended configuration is no file at all.
.Pp
A router on a non-standard SAM port, with downloads on a larger disk:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# clove.conf
sam_address 127.0.0.1:7000
data_dir /srv/clove
.Ed
.Pp
A throwaway identity for a one-off download:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ephemeral yes
.Ed
.Pp
Many torrents on a modest router: hold fewer streams overall, and stop any one
torrent from taking most of them.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
peer_limit 80
torrent_peer_limit 12
max_active_downloads 2
.Ed
.Pp
A system-wide instance with a fixed socket path, so a service manager and its
users agree on where to find it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
data_dir /var/lib/clove
api_socket /run/clove/clove.sock
.Ed
.Pp
Check a file before restarting the daemon.
A typo is fatal, and says where:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ cloved -C -c /etc/clove/clove.conf
config: line 3: unknown key "sam_adress"
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr clove 1 ,
.Xr clove-api 7 ,
.Xr cloved 8
